    "updated_at": "2022-11-02T02:46:53Z",
    "body": "Well, to be clear, by moving this from the one table to the other, it still needs to be supported as an event handler content attribute on `body`. The difference is that, when you do `<body onscrollend=\"foo(event)\">`, before this PR `event.target` would be `window`, and afterward it would be `document.body`.\r\n\r\nI agree `document.body` would be more normal, and so I support this PR. However, I think you might be confused about both its implications, and the test you're linking to. The test just seems incorrect to me. If you're scrolling the `body` element, then `<body onscrollend=\"foo(event)\">` should definitely work. There's no precedent for creating element event handlers that don't work as content attributes on body!",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
